-- title:
--   Iterated Uₚ of p-th powers: a Frobenius-twisted cusp form
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, let $L$ be a nonzero natural number, and let $v$ be a natural number with $2 \le v$ and with $v$ equal to the exponent of $p$ in the factorisation of $L$. Let $g$ be a cusp form of weight $2$ for $\Gamma_0(L)$ and suppose that every coefficient $\mathrm{qCoeff}\,g\,n$ — the $n$-th coefficient of the $q$-expansion of $g$ at width $1$ — lies in the integral closure of $\mathbb{Z}$ in $\mathbb{C}$, i.e. is an algebraic integer. The assertion is that there exists a cusp form $F$ of weight $2p^{v-1}$ for $\Gamma_0\bigl(p\cdot(L/p^{v})\bigr)$ with three properties: every $q$-expansion coefficient of $F$ is an algebraic integer; for every intermediate field $K$ of $\mathbb{C}/\mathbb{Q}$ that contains all coefficients of $g$, all coefficients of $F$ lie in $K$; and for every $n$ and all algebraic integers $a$, $A$ representing the $n$-th coefficients of $g$ and of $F$ respectively, the difference $A - a^{p^{v-1}}$ lies in the ideal generated by $p$ in the ring of algebraic integers. Thus $F$ is, coefficientwise modulo $p$, the $p^{v-1}$-st Frobenius power of $g$.
--
--   This is the iterated form of the step in Ribet's level-lowering argument that trades powers of $p$ in the level for weight: passing from $g$ to $U_p(g^p)$ divides one $p$ out of the level, multiplies the weight by $p$, and raises the $q$-coefficients to the $p$-th power modulo $p$. Here the process is run $v-1$ times, until exactly one factor $p$ remains in the level; the result feeds the construction of a Hecke-algebra ideal attached to a newform of level divisible by $p^2$.
